Durarara!! AU
Chapter 34 – Part 1
The door shut.
The helmet’s filtered breath was gone. The red cap sat on the back of the couch like a person who had stepped out of it. Two keys sat in two fists.
Anri looked at Rio.
“Why didn’t you tell him you were part of the Dollars too?”
Rio’s mouth opened. Closed. She dropped onto the couch hard enough to make the cap jump.
“Because I was going to.” A short, ugly laugh. “Then he walked out waving the same meeting in my face like weather and I forgot how to be a person.”
Anri sat opposite, careful, the Mika-name still warm in the room.
Rio dragged both hands through her hair.
“I joined before I met him. Before a rooftop. Before a key.” She stared at the dark phone. “It wasn’t a gang in my head. It was a chat. People who showed up when my house was full and I still had nowhere to stand. Handles. Stupid jokes. Urban legends. I wasn’t a Yellow Scarf. I wasn’t anybody’s soldier. I was just… in it.”
A thinner beat.
“If I say it out loud to him, I stop being the girl he pulled off a roof and I start being another color in a city he already reads like a crime scene. He clocked Kadota’s van in a night. He compared a forum ping to a cartel van. I didn’t want to watch his face do the file thing.”
Anri’s voice stayed soft.
“He gave you a key anyway.”
“I know.” Rio’s jaw worked. “That’s worse. He handed me a door and I let him leave thinking the summons was only his.” She glanced at Anri. “You didn’t out me.”
“It wasn’t mine to out.” Anri looked at the bedroom door he had come through in a cap and left in a mask. “I have pieces he asked me to carry quietly. Mika. A plan. A sidewalk I wasn’t allowed to turn around on. I understand keeping a room to yourself. I don’t understand keeping it when he’s walking into the same room tonight.”
Rio flinched.
“You think I should have said me too while he was putting a demon on his face.”
“I think he told us not to go because he doesn’t want us in a crowd he can’t see the edges of.” Anri’s fingers tightened on the key. “If you’re already in the crowd… he should know which edge you’re standing on. Not because he owns you. Because he just said come back to us is the job.”
Rio stared at the cap.
“…He doesn’t even know who kicked the hive,” she muttered. Then, quieter, honest: “Neither do I. It’s just a meeting. It could be nothing. It could be the thing he felt in his bones.”
Anri did not say Mikado.
She did not have Mikado.
She had a first-year who blushed and said Anri-chan and got jealous of a class-rep hand in a hallway. That was not a founder. That was a boy.
“Then we stay,” Anri said. “Like we promised. And when he comes back, you tell him. Before the city tells him first.”
Rio’s mouth pulled.
“You’re very calm for someone who just heard Mika Harima is alive.”
Anri’s eyes shone once. She let it.
“I have been calm about worse things in my own head.” A small breath. “Tonight I get to be calm about a friend coming back to school. That is enough. The Dollars can wait on this couch.”
Rio looked at her. Really looked.
“…You’re in love with him.”
Anri did not deny it.
“So are you,” she said, just as quiet. “I’m not going to make it a fight. Not in his kitchen. Not while he’s wearing that thing toward a meeting he compared to a cage.”
Rio swallowed. Nodded once, like the exception Anri had already filed had been spoken aloud and survived.
The apartment ticked. The kettle sat unused. Two phones stayed dark on purpose.
On a furnished floor that had chosen to wait,
Anri Sonohara asked the question the mask had not had time for.
Rio Kamichika had a color and no founder’s name — only a chat
she had loved because it was not her parents.
Neither girl knew the hive had been kicked by a first-year
with a broken door. They only knew Curtis Nightstar was walking toward it undead.
Rio blew out a breath that was almost a laugh.
“Sheesh, Anri-chan. Well. We are girls, right?”
She said it like the love-talk could be set down on the table next to the keys and survive being ignored for ten minutes. Her hand found the remote on the low stand. The 55-inch smart TV woke with a soft click.
The channel was already USB.
The screen filled with folders.
Not streaming. Not a homepage. A life dumped into rectangles.
UNDEAD MUSIC
RANDOM VIDS BY LEONA
MEXICO
JUSTICE
KATLYN
and then others — dates, cities, names Anri did not know how to pronounce on first sight. A folder that was only a string of numbers. One labeled LIST that made Anri’s stomach tick without knowing why.
Rio stared.
“Woah.”
Anri sat forward despite herself.
The television was huge in the quiet apartment. Every title looked like a door he had not handed them with the keys.
“…He leaves this on the USB,” Anri said, soft. “Like it isn’t a museum.”
Rio thumbed down one line and stopped before opening anything.
“Undead music. That’s the song. The street one. Keep it undead. He said it like a vow.” Her eyes flicked to JUSTICE. The name they both had now, in different amounts. “And that’s his brother.”
Anri’s gaze caught on MEXICO and would not stay there. Cartel. Capture. The sentence he had used to bench them for a meeting.
Then KATLYN.
Rio made a face.
“That one feels like an ex.”
Anri did not disagree. She looked at RANDOM VIDS BY LEONA the way she looked at a person she had not been introduced to.
“Leona,” she repeated. “He said there were others. After Justice. People who don’t know he’s here.”
The cursor sat blinking on the list, a small white accusation.
Rio’s thumb hovered.
“If we open one, we’re the kind of girls who go through a boy’s hard drive because he gave us a key and a helmet and a name.”
Anri’s mouth pulled.
“If we don’t, we sit here imagining worse things than what’s in the folders.” A beat. “He said unpack later. This is unpacking without him in the room.”
Rio set the remote against her knee. Did not click.
The 55-inch glow painted both of them in other people’s titles.
“Sheesh,” Rio said again, quieter. “We are girls. That doesn’t mean we raid the dead brother.”
Anri nodded once.
Her eyes stayed on JUSTICE anyway. Then on LIST. Then on the music folder that had already walked into Ikebukuro through a borrowed guitar.
On a television that had been left
like an unlocked drawer, Rio Kamichika found a country of names he had not introduced.
Anri Sonohara saw Mexico and a list and a girl called Katlyn and did not press play.
The Dollars meeting was still happening somewhere in the city.
In the apartment, the Undead were only folders.
Rio’s thumb lost the argument.
“Random vids by Leona,” she said. “That’s not the dead brother. That’s… a person with a camera.”
Anri did not stop her.
The folder opened on a grid of thumbnails. Concert lighting. A kitchen at 2 a.m. A parking lot and tall cans. A crooked shot of a talent-show curtain. A still of Curtis smiling so hard it did not look like the boy who had just sealed a red circle over his face.
Rio tapped that one.
The title was only a year.
Curtis would have been seventeen.
The 55-inch filled with a fuselage.
It was a plane — cheap seats, too-small windows, the kind of compact that made two tall boys look like they had been packed on purpose. Someone’s knee in the aisle. A seatbelt light. The camera was handheld and fond.
Off-screen, a girl’s voice, laughing, American-easy:
“Stop posing. It’s a red-eye, not a premiere.”
“Leona, I look good,” Curtis said into the lens, and it was him — same braids, same mismatched eyes — except the mouth was doing something the apartment version had forgotten how to do. Upbeat. Bright. No cartel in the shoulders yet. No list sitting like a blade in a pocket.
Beside him, a boy with a grin that took up more oxygen than the cabin allowed leaned into frame and flicked Curtis’s forehead.
“You look like a golden retriever who found a window.”
“Justice,” Curtis said, delighted, like the name was a punchline they shared. “You’re on camera.”
“I’m always on camera. Leona documents war crimes.” Justice squinted at the lens. “Hi Leona. Stop filming my brother like he’s a nature special.”
The camera shook with the girl’s laugh.
“Say hi to the future, idiots.”
Curtis did. He waved. Seventeen. Alive in a way that made the furnished quiet feel late.
“Future,” he said, mock-solemn, then broke. “We’re going to eat terrible airport food and live forever. Keep it undead.”
Justice bumped his shoulder, easy, unbreakable.
“Keep it undead. Don’t crash the plane, Leona.”
“I’m not flying it, you walnut.”
The clip ran forty seconds and ended on Curtis still smiling, window-light on his face, Justice’s hand hooked around the back of his neck like leaving was a joke they had not learned yet.
The TV went back to the pause screen.
Rio did not speak.
Anri’s eyes were wet and angry about it.
“…That’s him,” Rio said at last, rough. “That’s the same person. It’s just— he had a setting we don’t get.”
Anri nodded once.
“Justice is loud.” Soft. Helpless. “He looks at Curtis like Curtis is the safest thing in the air.”
Rio swallowed.
“Thirty days,” she muttered, not sure who she was telling. “He said he left without saying goodbye. Those people still think he’s in a forest. They’re going to see a plane video one day and not know the window is Japan.”
Anri looked at the filename row. Other thumbnails waited. Carlos was not in this one. Maria was not in this one. A German boy and a Jewish girl were only a laugh behind the camera and a talent-show still in the grid.
The Undead, on a USB, were still a crew that fit in a cabin.
In Ikebukuro, two of the seats were already empty.
Rio set the remote down like it had gotten heavy.
“We should stop.”
Anri did not reach for the next file.
She looked at the paused smile instead — seventeen, undead, going somewhere with his brother — and heard the filtered helmet voice from ten minutes ago promising Mika like it was the only mercy he could afford.
“Yes,” she said. “We should stop.”
They did not stop looking at the screen.
On a 55-inch that had been left
like an unlocked drawer,
Leona’s camera
gave them a red-eye
and a boy who still knew
how to wave
at the future.
Rio Kamichika saw the setting
Ikebukuro did not get.
Anri Sonohara saw Justice alive and understood the list a little better.
The meeting kept happening without them.
The plane stayed paused.
The pause screen lied.
Rio’s thumb twitched. The clip kept going.
A woman leaned into the lens from the seat across, smile bright enough to look expensive even in a cabin.
“So, babe. Going to do your crazy thing and jump out the plane?”
The air in the footage changed.
Not the altitude. The people.
A boy with a sun-warmed face — Carlos, if the later filenames were going to be believed — stopped grinning. A girl tucked against his side — Maria — glanced at the woman and then at Curtis like she was checking a fuse. Off-camera, Leona’s laugh thinned. Someone with a dry German mutter — Adolf — said something the mic only half-caught, and it was not a joke about a name. It was the sound of a crew that did not like the girl in the aisle and had agreed, badly, not to say so in front of Curtis.
Curtis did not catch it.
Seventeen. Upbeat. Her babe landing on him like weather he had paid for.
“Yeah, babe. One more thing Justice and I can cross off.”
Justice’s face did the opposite of the others. He grabbed Curtis by the jacket and hauled.
“Then let’s fuckin’ go. Drop your grenades, you soft dove.”
The camera lurched.
Wind. Door. The stupid beautiful noise of a plane giving people back to the sky.
Carlos went out yelling, bright and fearless.
“¡Órale, homes!”
Adolf followed like a man keeping a promise he would complain about for the rest of his life.
“Why do I let you all talk me into this?”
Leona, still filming, voice fond and sharp in the same breath:
“Stop being a bitch, love.”
Maria’s laugh tore out of her, high and delighted.
“Weeee—!”
Justice:
“Bro!”
Curtis, already gone from the cabin, from the girl, from the list for one clean second:
“Let’s do this!”
The lens found them in freefall.
Two bodies that had grown up crashing through a forest like the trees were a game. High-speed flips. Tight, showing off, synchronized in the way only people who had survived the same hunger could be. Braids whipping. Justice laughing with his whole mouth. Curtis smiling like the ground was optional.
Leona, breathless behind the camera:
“Those show-offs.”
The clip cut on blue sky and two brothers who had not learned the ending yet.
The 55-inch held the last frame.
Rio sat back slowly.
“…That’s the whole crew,” she said. “That’s six. He wasn’t a wanderer with one ghost. He was a circus.”
Anri’s eyes were on the empty seat the woman had occupied.
“They didn’t like her.” Quiet. Certain. “Everyone in that plane except him.”
Rio nodded once.
“Katlyn folder.” The name tasted like a warning label. “He called her babe. They looked at her like she was going to steal the jump.”
Anri swallowed.
“And Justice still pulled him out. Soft dove. Grenades.” The song from the first night sat down beside the video and made sense in a worse way. “That wasn’t poetry. That was them.”
Rio let out a long breath.
“Carlos. Maria. Adolf. Leona with the camera. All still somewhere that isn’t here. And that girl…” She glanced at Anri. “That’s the California one. Has to be. The one he won’t talk about.”
Anri looked at paused sky.
Seventeen-year-old Curtis spinning like joy was a skill.
Nineteen-year-old Curtis in a demon helmet walking toward a meeting he had compared to a cage.
“He was happy,” Anri said, and it sounded like an accusation aimed at the city, not at him. “He knew how.”
Rio set the remote down for good.
“Yeah. Then the plane landed.”
On a USB that had not asked permission, Leona’s camera gave them a door in the sky
and a girl the crew already mistrusted.
Carlos yelled. Adolf complained. Maria flew. Justice called him a dove and threw him at the list.
Curtis Nightstar, seventeen, flipped like the ground could not keep him.
In the apartment, two girls finally saw the Undead move.
They clicked out of the sky.
The folder list sat there again, innocent as a table of contents.
Rio’s voice came smaller.
“I’m… curious about the Katlyn one.”
Anri’s answer was barely air.
“I—I am too.”
The KATLYN folder opened on a handful of files. Dates. One still of a blonde at the edge of a frame, cropped like someone had already gotten tired of her face. The one in the middle was labeled, blunt as a bruise:
BLACK DAHLIA
Curtis, eighteen. Sitting on a crate in what looked like a garage that had been turned into a practice room. Hood down. Braids forward. No mask. No cap. A notebook open on his knee like the song had been a private thing first.
He looked at the camera the way a person looked at a jury of friends.
“Justice. That’s fucked up, man. I wrote this as a journal entry. And you all turned it into a song.”
Off-frame, Carlos, warm and done being gentle:
“Homes, we warned you for the last few years that puta was messed.”
Adolf, careful with the shape of the insult, German consonants sanded down so the word would not come out as cruel as he felt:
“I’m sorry, bro. But Carlos is right. I wish he wasn’t. She is… not a good person.”
A pause. The politeness sat there like a clenched fist he refused to swing.
Justice leaned in from the side, guitar already in his hands.
“C’mon, man. Sing for us. We’ll run it back on the instruments. I’ll do secondary guitar.”
Curtis exhaled. Eighteen. Still trying to be the upbeat boy from the plane and failing in public.
“Alright, guys.”
The room behind the lens counted in.
He sang.
Not the street-mask voice Ikebukuro had clapped for. This one was stripped. Journal-close. A little ashamed of how true it was.
I loved you, you made me, hate me
You gave me hate, see?
It saved me and these tears are deadly
You feel that I rip back, every time you tried to steal that
You feel bad, you feel sad?
I’m sorry, hell no, fuck that
It was my heart, it was my life, it was my start, it was your knife
This strife, it dies, this life and these lies
And these lungs have sung this song for too long
And it’s true, I hurt too, remember I loved you
The garage held him. Justice’s guitar came in under the second pass, loyal as a second spine. Someone — Leona, probably — kept the camera still like moving it would be a betrayal.
And I’ve lost it all, fell today, it’s all the same
I’m sorry oh, I’m sorry, I’m sorry, no
And I’ve been abused, I feel so used, because of you
I’m sorry oh, I’m sorry, I’m sorry, no
Rio’s hand found the couch cushion and stayed there.
Anri did not blink.
I wish I could have quit you, I wish I never missed you
And told you that I loved you, every time I fucked you
The future that we both drew, and all the shit we’ve been through
Obsessed with the thought of you, the pain just grew and grew
How could you do this to me?
Look at what I made for you, it never was enough and the world is what I gave to you
I used to be love struck, now I’m just fucked up
Pull up my sleeves and see the pattern of my cuts
Anri’s breath caught on that line.
He had told her the scars were stories. Animals. People. A forest. Not a journal he sang at friends. The song did not have to be a map. It still sat in the room like one.
Seems like all we had is over now, you left to rest
And your tears are dried up now, you just lay without a sound
Seems like all we had is over now, you left to rest
And my fears are over now
I can leave with my head down
He said I’m sorry until the word stopped meaning apology and started meaning a door he could not get back through.
The last no landed. Justice’s guitar rang once and died.
Eighteen-year-old Curtis stared at the crate between his boots.
“That’s the entry,” he muttered. “You happy?”
Justice, off-camera, rough and fond:
“No. But it’s honest. Keep it undead anyway.”
The file ended.
The apartment did not know what to do with the quiet.
Rio spoke first, eyes on the black screen.
“…That’s the babe from the plane.” Not a question. “That’s why they looked at her like a fuse.”
Anri’s voice was thin.
“He loved her. She used it. They tried to tell him for years.” She swallowed. “He still called her babe in the air.”
Rio dragged a hand down her face.
“And then he flew to Japan and handed us keys like we weren’t walking into a house that already had a song like that in it.”
Anri looked at the remote. Did not pick it up.
“We shouldn’t open another one.”
“Yeah.” Rio’s laugh came out wrong. “We are girls. We are also nosy disasters.” A beat. “He said he was a wreck when he told you he liked you. That video is the wreck with a melody.”
Anri nodded.
The unpaid sentence from the sidewalk — you’re not a freak to me — sat next to a journal that had been turned into a chorus.
On a 55-inch in a flat he had not finished unpacking,
Katlyn became a folder and a knife and a song the Undead had refused
to let him keep private. Rio Kamichika learned the California girl had a warning attached.
Anri Sonohara heard I’m sorry until it stopped sounding like her name.
The Dollars meeting was still happening.
The garage had already closed.
The file was not over.
Maria leaned into frame, Korean-accented English dry as tape over a wound, eyes on Justice first.
“So? Justice. Did you take liberties on Curtis’s scars?” A flick of her fingers at the notebook, at the line that had just sat in the room like a confession. “You both didn’t get those from cutting yourselves.”
Justice’s laugh came bright and unhelpful.
“Hahaha, yeah. But hey — Curtis sounded so emotional.”
Curtis moved.
One arm hooked around Justice’s neck, no heat in it except embarrassment. A brother-chokehold. The crate scraped. The notebook hit the floor.
“You little fucker—”
They went over sideways in a tangle of braids and guitar cables and the kind of violence that had never been meant to end anyone. The camera lurched after them through the garage door into a slab of daylight. Two bodies hitting concrete on purpose. High-impact fists that slapped more than they punished. Justice wheezing laughter. Curtis laughing too — really laughing, the plane-smile back for a second like the journal had not just come out of his mouth.
They looked like boys who had survived a forest and a girl and a list and still knew how to knock each other into a wall for fun.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes were alive.
Justice’s grin was the same one that had flicked his forehead at thirty thousand feet.
The lens caught the wind-up — both of them about to put a fist in the other’s face, joy written all the way through it—
—and cut.
Black screen.
USB menu.
Rio let out a breath she had been holding since pattern of my cuts.
“…Okay.” Rough. Relieved. “Okay. Maria said it. That’s not what the lyric was doing.”
Anri’s shoulders dropped by a degree she felt in her neck.
He had told her the scars were stories. Animals. People. A forest. The song had made the line sound like a map she was not allowed to have. Maria had put the map back in the drawer.
“They turned his journal into a hook,” Anri said, quiet. “Justice made it worse on purpose. Then they went outside and hit each other because that’s how they say I love you.”
Rio huffed a weak laugh.
“That’s the most boy thing I have ever seen after the most girl-destroying song I have ever sat through.”
On the dark glass, the last frame still sat behind their eyes: two brothers about to collide, happy, undead, not in Ikebukuro, not in a helmet, not walking toward a meeting that felt like a cage.
Anri looked at the cap on the couch.
“That’s the setting,” she whispered. “The one we don’t get. Not because he won’t give it. Because the other half of it is gone.”
Rio nodded once, throat tight.
“Thirty days,” she said again. “And he still laughs like that in a file. Then he puts a demon on his face and tells us to stay on the couch.”
Neither of them pressed play on the next thing.
The Undead could keep the rest of the garage.
On a 55-inch that had already taken too much, Maria told the truth about the scars.
Justice stole a lyric and paid for it in a headlock.
Curtis Nightstar and the boy who had promised not to leave
hit the daylight laughing. The video ended before either fist could land.
In the apartment, two girls put the remote down for a moment.